// FoldConstants — a Std->Std pass that EVALUATES `const <op> const`, replacing the arithmetic with the
// answer. `2 + 3 * 4` stops being `mov`/`imul`/`lea` and becomes one `const 14`; a `condBranch` whose
// condition is a known constant becomes an unconditional `branch`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS A Std PASS AND NOT A PARSER ONE, AND THE POST-INLINE HALF IS THE BIGGER HALF.** The parser
// already settles `const <op> const` written as such — `2 + 3 * 4` never reaches this pass at all. What
// reaches it is what INLINING makes constant: `inlineLeaves` substitutes a literal argument into a
// callee's body, so `x * 31 + y` called with `(3, 4)` becomes `const 3 * const 31 + const 4` in the
// caller — three constant expressions the parser could not have seen, because at parse time they were
// parameters. That is the shape this pass exists for, and it is why it is scheduled after BOTH inliners.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE ARITHMETIC IS NOT THIS PASS'S OWN. IT IS THE PARSER'S CONSTANT FOLDER, ASKED.**
// `TypeRules.foldIntBinOp`, `TypeRules.evalShift` and `TypeRules.foldIntCompare` are where this compiler
// decides what `const <op> const` EVALUATES TO, and they had exactly one caller — `Parser.recordBinOpFold`,
// which computes the answer and RECORDS it in the constant domain without rewriting the op. This pass is
// the second caller, and it rewrites. A Std-tier evaluator of its own would have been a second answer to
// one question, which is this project's signature bug (`.claude/CLAUDE.md` opens with it) and is exactly
// how `Parser.recordBinOpFold`'s own header says a diagnostic was lost once already.
//
// The two tiers speak different opcode sets, so the bridge is `maxonBinOpOfStdOpcode` /
// `maxonCmpOpOfStdPred` — CHECKED INVERSES of the lowering's own maps, which panic rather than answer if
// the two directions ever diverge. See their header for why they live beside the forward map.
//
// ⭐ **WHY THE ANSWER IS THE SAME AT BOTH TIERS, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE SHARING SOUND.** A `ParsedInt` is
// a signed 64-bit integer, and EVERY integer `binOp` and `cmp` in this compiler computes at the MACHINE
// WORD on all three targets — x64 selects 64-bit register forms and reads no width at all, arm64 selects
// the X-register forms, and wasm computes at `wasmMachineValTypeOf`, which is `i64` for every integer
// type. A Std `operandType` is therefore a STORAGE class here and never an arithmetic one, and the host
// arithmetic the folder runs is bit-for-bit the arithmetic the instruction would have run — WRAPPING
// included: `i64.max + 1` folds to `i64.min`, which is what `add` produces. That argument covers
// ARITHMETIC completely, because add/sub/mul/and/or/xor are bit-identical under both readings.
//
// ⛔ **IT DOES NOT COVER COMPARISONS, AND THIS HEADER ONCE CLAIMED IT DID.** It used to say every
// integer comparison is SIGNED at 64 bits on all three targets, so `foldIntCompare` was the whole
// answer. X11 made signedness a real distinction carried on the op (`stdTypeIsUnsigned`), and the two
// readings of one bit pattern disagree: `u64.max` rides as the wrapped `-1`. An unsigned comparison is
// therefore DECLINED — see `evaluateCmp`, which carries the measurement.
//
// FIVE THINGS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT FOLDED, each for its own reason:
//
//   • **`div`/`mod` — they TRAP.** They are not even `binOp`s: integer division has its own `StdOp.div`/
//     `StdOp.mod` variants carrying `isPure: false`, because x64 `idiv` faults on a zero divisor and on
//     `i64.min / -1`. `classifyArithOperands` therefore never offers one as a subject, and the prune
//     below (`dceRetiredDefs`) retires only `const`s, so neither can be dropped for being unread either.
//     ⚠ Note that `foldIntDivision` is nonetheless RIGHT about the pair a division can and cannot answer
//     — `i64.min / -1` has no representable quotient and declines, `i64.min mod -1` is `0` and folds —
//     which is the A1x finding, and this pass rests on it rather than restating it.
//   • **A FLOAT ANSWER THE HOST AND THE TARGET COULD SPELL DIFFERENTLY** — see the float band below,
//     which is three named declines rather than a blanket one. Floats DO fold as of 2026-08-30.
//   • **SHIFT COUNTS OUTSIDE THE UNGUARDED WINDOW** (`TypeRules.shiftCountIsUnguarded`, 0..63). See
//     `evaluateIntBinOp` — the window is asked, not restated.
//   • **AN UNSIGNED COMPARISON** — `stdTypeIsUnsigned(operandType)`. See `evaluateCmp`: one bit
//     pattern, two honest readings, and this compiler's only integer-comparison folder speaks the
//     signed one.
//   • **THE SIGN-EXTENSION FAMILY** — the only unary opcodes left that never fold, and there is nothing
//     to fold: `Parser.emitSignExtendFromSlot` is the sole emit site and its operand is the result of a
//     runtime element-load CALL, which this pass never holds a constant for. Every OTHER unary shape
//     folds as of 2026-08-31 — `neg`/`bitNot`/`logicalNot`, the two conversions, the six f64→f64
//     intrinsics and both reinterpretations (`evaluateUnaryOp` and its two domain arms). The look that
//     `neg` fold once owed `InsertRangeChecks.recordConstNeg` has been taken: that pass runs FIRST
//     (`PassPipeline`), folding a `neg` for its own constant domain while the op is still an op, so the
//     two never see each other's work — and both spell the same `-x`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **FLOATS FOLD, AND THE RULE IS: THE HOST'S BITS ARE THE INSTRUCTION'S BITS, OR THE OPERATION
// KEEPS ITS INSTRUCTION.** A float constant is a `ParsedInt` holding the double's IEEE-754 BIT PATTERN
// on the same `StdOp.const` an integer uses (float-ness is the op's `valueType`, not a second opcode —
// v1's `constI64`/`constF64` split is deliberately merged here), so the REWRITE is identical and the
// only new question is whether the compiling host computes what the target's instruction would. For
// `+ - * /`, `min`/`max`, every comparison, `floor`/`ceil`/`round`/`sqrt`/`abs`/`fneg` and the int↔float
// conversions and reinterpretations, it does: IEEE-754 fixes each to ONE answer and shv2 emits that one
// answer on all three targets. The evaluators are `TypeRules`' own, exactly as the integer ones are.
//
// **THE THREE ORIGINAL DEFERRAL REASONS, ANSWERED:** *signed zero* — the host produces the instruction's
// zero because it runs the instruction (`-0.0 + 0.0` is `+0.0` on the folder and on `addsd` alike), and
// the sign operations are bit work rather than arithmetic (`NumberParsing.negatedFloatBits`); *NaN
// payloads* — declined outright, below; *shortest-round-trip printing* — a folded constant IS the bits
// the instruction would have produced, so a program prints the same text either way. That last one was
// never a reason to refuse the fold; it was a reason to require the first two.
//
// **THREE DECLINES, EACH ITS OWN:**
//
//   • **A DIVISION BY A CONSTANT `±0.0`.** `foldFloatBinOp` rides the HOST's own trap rather than bit-
//     testing the divisor (a bit test reads `-0.0` as the ordinary number `i64.min`), and answers
//     `notFoldable`. Symmetric with the integer `div`/`mod` band above and with the emitted checked
//     divide, whose zero flag is a VALUE compare and so catches both zeros.
//   • **A COMPUTED ANSWER THAT IS A NaN.** `foldedFloatConst` states the rule and the payload argument
//     behind it; do not restate either here. `inf - inf` and `sqrt(-4.0)` keep their instructions.
//   • **A `fpToSi` WITH NO `i64` ANSWER** (`TypeRules.foldFpToSi`). The three targets diverge outside
//     the range and wasm's `i64.trunc_f64_s` TRAPS there; folding would erase the trap.
//
// ⚠ **THE MINTED `const`'s `valueType` IS THE RESULT'S DOMAIN AT ITS MACHINE WORD, NEVER THE OP'S
// `operandType`** — see `FoldedFloatType`. On the conversions and the bitcasts those two are different
// types, and getting it backwards is the measured x64 register-file failure the float control pins.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT A CONSTANT PROPAGATOR.** It reads only what a `const` op materializes; a value that is
// constant on every path into a phi is not constant to this pass, and nothing here looks through memory
// (`classifyFoldableDef`'s memory band states why a load's result is not its global's initializer).
//
// ⭐ **WHY THE OLD REASON FOR NOT HAVING THIS PASS IS GONE.** `FoldConstOperands`' header, `DEVLOG.md`
// and `Parser.recordBinOpFold` all said the same thing: folding every constant expression to a single
// `mov` would erase the codegen the spec fragments exist to show. Goldens became REFERENCE rather than a
// gate on 2026-08-27 (user ruling, `1152bbc566`), so that argument no longer holds — and the specs whose
// SUBJECT is the emitted arithmetic were rewritten to take a runtime input, which is strictly better
// testing than a literal a correct compiler is entitled to fold.

// Fold `const <op> const` throughout `module`, in place.
//
// It runs over the WHOLE module, and unlike `foldConstOperands` it takes no start index: its other
// caller would be the appended runtime band, and the hand-built runtime bodies (`MmRuntime`,
// `GtRuntime`, the synthesized cascades) contain no `const <op> const` at all — every arithmetic op
// they emit has at least one live operand. A second run there would walk every appended function to
// fold nothing.
export function foldConstants(module StdModule)
	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "foldConstants: {module.funcCount()} function(s)")
	// An INDEX loop, not `for … in`: the element is a BORROW of `module`, and the call below is
	// recorded as writing that same storage. ⚖ Ruling of 2026-08-26 — canonical note at
	// `IR/Std/ElimTrivialBlockArgs.elimTrivialBlockArgs`.
	for fnIndex in 0 upto module.functions.count() 'eachFunc'
		foldConstantsInFunc(module, func: try module.functions.get(fnIndex) otherwise panic("foldConstants: fnIndex {fnIndex} is below module.functions.count()"))
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'foldConstants'

// ⭐ **ONE FORWARD WALK IS ENOUGH, AND THAT IS A PROPERTY OF THE IR RATHER THAN AN APPROXIMATION.** Each
// op is folded and then RECLASSIFIED, so the `const` it just became is in the map before the next op
// reads it — which is what collapses `const 3 * const 31 + const 4` to one `const 97` in a single pass
// without a worklist or a fixpoint.
//
// It leans on defs preceding uses, which `liveOpIndices` gives for a parser-generated CFG (blocks in
// `func.blockRefs` LAYOUT order). The cost of a miss is exactly a MISSED FOLD: an operand whose `const`
// has not been walked yet reads as non-constant and the op is left alone. There is no arrangement in
// which a wrong constant is produced — the same argument, for the same reason, that
// `foldConstOperands.substituteIdentityImmediates` makes about applying a substitution in walk order.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND THE ORDER IS DEMONSTRABLY WRONG FOR SOME BLOCKS, WHICH IS WHY THE PRICE IS MEASURED RATHER
// THAN ASSUMED.** Every block-splitting pass APPENDS its new blocks to `func.blockRefs`, so a block's
// position there records WHEN IT WAS MINTED, not where it sits in the CFG. MEASURED on
// `return (opaque(3) * 4 + 2) as ExitCode`, the order is `entry, __rc_ok, __rc_panic, __rc_chk,
// __il_cont, __il_body` — `insertRangeChecks` ran first so its blocks come first, and the `const` the
// arithmetic folds to is minted in `__il_cont`, three blocks LATER than the range-check compare that
// would fold against it. That compare therefore does not fold, and the guard survives.
//
// ⭐ **A FIXPOINT WOULD CURE IT AND IS NOT WORTH IT — MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED.** Re-running the walk until
// it converges (8 rounds, forced) against ONE round, on the committed `scripts/emitted-code-count.py`
// corpus: **12,189 emitted ops against 12,191** — two ops, both in `probe.maxon`, and byte-identical
// everywhere else. The exact cure is a def-before-use order (reverse postorder of the Std CFG) or a
// sparse def-use worklist, either of which is a mechanism of its own; `EC13`'s CSE needs the first one
// anyway, and that is the rung to build it in. Iterating this walk instead would trade a LINEAR pass for
// one whose worst case is O(blocks x ops), for two ops.
function foldConstantsInFunc(module StdModule, func IrFunction)
	let opIndices = module.liveOpIndices(func)
	var constMap = ConstValueMap.create()

	evaluateOps(module, opIndices: opIndices, constMap: constMap)
	// AFTER the op walk, because a `cmp` this pass folded is exactly how a branch condition becomes a
	// known constant, and BEFORE the prune, which is what retires the condition `const` the branch
	// fold stops reading.
	//
	// A fold ORPHANS the arm it did not take, and this pass must remove it itself — see
	// `dropUnreachableBlocks` for the allocator invariant that says so.
	if foldConstantBranches(module, func: func, constMap: constMap) 'branchesFolded'
		dropUnreachableBlocks(module, func: func)
	end 'branchesFolded'

	// The `const`s this pass detached from their readers — the operands of every op it replaced, and the
	// condition of every branch it made unconditional. Shared with `foldConstOperands` rather than
	// re-derived: it is the same narrow rule (retire a `const` nothing reads), and the pass that owns it
	// states at length why it must not grow into a general DCE.
	//
	// ⚠ Its other half — retiring an unreferenced IDENTITY `binOpImm` — is inert here and that is not a
	// coincidence to rely on: `foldConstOperands` is the only producer of an immediate form and it runs
	// AFTER this pass, so the pipeline reaches this call with none in the module. Should one ever arrive,
	// dropping it is still correct (it is pure and unread); what would be missing is the SUBSTITUTION
	// that gives its readers somewhere else to look, and an op with readers is never retired.
	dceRetiredDefs(module, func: func, opIndices: opIndices, def: StdFoldableDef.create())
end 'foldConstantsInFunc'

// Replace every foldable op with the `const` it evaluates to, in place at the same op index (so block
// `opRefs` and terminator slots stay valid), recording each constant as it appears.
//
// The store is UNCONDITIONAL for `rewriteBinAndCmpOps`' measured reason: `tryEvaluate` hands back the op
// that belongs at the index, so an op that does not fold is written back over itself — a refcount and no
// allocation, where a union saying "unchanged" would be a heap box per op.
function evaluateOps(module StdModule, opIndices OpIndexArray, constMap ConstValueMap)
	// ONE record of each kind for the whole walk, refilled per op and never re-created — see
	// `StdArithOperands` and `StdFoldableDef` for why both answers are written into caller-owned records.
	var operands = StdArithOperands.create()
	var def = StdFoldableDef.create()

	for opIdx in opIndices 'eachOp'
		let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("foldConstants: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
		let folded = tryEvaluate(op, constMap: constMap, operands: operands)
		try module.ops.set(opIdx, value: folded) otherwise panic("foldConstants: ops.set OOB at {opIdx}")

		// The FOLDED op, not the original — recording the result is what feeds the next op's operands.
		classifyFoldableDef(folded, def: def)
		if def.isConstDef() 'isConst'
			constMap.upsert(def.definedId, value: def.constant)
		end 'isConst'
	end 'eachOp'
end 'evaluateOps'

// Attempt to evaluate one op, answering with THE OP THAT BELONGS AT ITS INDEX — a `const` carrying the
// computed value, or `op` itself where nothing folds.
//
// Which ops are subjects at all is `classifyArithOperands`' answer, shared with `foldConstOperands`:
// that roster is enumerated once. This pass acts on three of its shapes — the two-operand arithmetic
// and comparison it shares with `foldConstOperands`, and the ONE-operand `unaryOp`, which that pass
// never touches because no target has an immediate form of a negate.
function tryEvaluate(op StdOp, constMap ConstValueMap, operands StdArithOperands) returns StdOp
	classifyArithOperands(op, operands: operands)

	if operands.isBinaryArith() 'arith'
		return evaluateBinOp(op, resultId: operands.resultId, lhs: operands.lhs, rhs: operands.rhs, opcode: operands.opcode, operandType: operands.operandType, constMap: constMap)
	end 'arith'

	if operands.isComparison() 'comparison'
		return evaluateCmp(op, resultId: operands.resultId, lhs: operands.lhs, rhs: operands.rhs, pred: operands.pred, operandType: operands.operandType, constMap: constMap)
	end 'comparison'

	if operands.isUnary() 'unary'
		return evaluateUnaryOp(op, resultId: operands.resultId, operand: operands.lhs, opcode: operands.unaryOpcode, operandType: operands.operandType, constMap: constMap)
	end 'unary'

	return op
end 'tryEvaluate'

// ⭐ **THE ADMISSION RULE FOR THE INTEGER PATH, ASKED BY ALL THREE EVALUATORS.** Arithmetic, comparison
// and the unary fold ask exactly the same two questions of an operand and would have answered them in
// three places, which is the duplication this file is otherwise at pains to avoid.
//
// ⚠ **THE FLOAT REFUSAL STAYS EVEN THOUGH FLOATS NOW FOLD, AND IT IS NOT REDUNDANT.** Each evaluator
// DISPATCHES on `stdTypeIsFloat(operandType)` and routes a float to its own admission rule and its own
// evaluator, so nothing reaches here with a float in the ordinary course. What this refusal is, is the
// backstop for the one mistake that cannot be seen in a test: a float operand arriving at the INTEGER
// folder, which would combine two IEEE-754 bit patterns as if they were numbers and write the answer
// back as an integer `const`. That is not a wrong number, it is a value in the wrong register file —
// `x64 emitter: rax is in the gpr register file where the xmm file is required` (MEASURED 2026-08-28).
function operandIsFoldableInteger(constMap ConstValueMap, operand ValueId, operandType StdType) returns bool
	if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) 'float'
		return false
	end 'float'

	return constMap.contains(operand)
end 'operandIsFoldableInteger'

// The two-operand form of the rule: `operandType` is the type BOTH operands are read at, so the float
// guard is asked once and the second operand adds only its own constancy.
function operandsAreFoldableIntegers(constMap ConstValueMap, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, operandType StdType) returns bool
	return operandIsFoldableInteger(constMap, operand: lhs, operandType: operandType) and constMap.contains(rhs)
end 'operandsAreFoldableIntegers'

// ⭐ **THE FLOAT TWIN, AND ITS TYPE QUESTION IS THE OPPOSITE ONE.** The integer rule asks "is this NOT a
// float"; this asks "is this the float WIDTH the folders compute at". `TypeRules`' float evaluators run
// on the host's native f64 throughout, so an `f32` operand — which `StdType` can spell and nothing in
// this compiler mints (a Maxon `float` is an f64, which `requireF64` and `requireScalarDouble` say at
// every backend) — would be computed at the wrong width silently. Asking for `FoldedFloatType` by name
// is the same question the minting side answers, so the two cannot drift apart.
function operandIsFoldableFloat(constMap ConstValueMap, operand ValueId, operandType StdType) returns bool
	if operandType != FoldedFloatType 'notTheHostsDouble'
		return false
	end 'notTheHostsDouble'

	return constMap.contains(operand)
end 'operandIsFoldableFloat'

// The two-operand form, shaped exactly like `operandsAreFoldableIntegers`: `operandType` is the type
// BOTH operands are read at, so the width is asked once and the second operand adds only its constancy.
function operandsAreFoldableFloats(constMap ConstValueMap, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, operandType StdType) returns bool
	return operandIsFoldableFloat(constMap, operand: lhs, operandType: operandType) and constMap.contains(rhs)
end 'operandsAreFoldableFloats'

// ⭐⭐ **THE `const` A COMPUTED FLOAT ANSWER BECOMES — OR THE OP, WHERE THAT ANSWER IS A NaN.** Both
// float evaluators mint through here, and it is the ONE statement of the NaN decline: a generated NaN's
// payload is the machine's choice, so a folded one would carry the compiling host's bits into a binary
// the target's own instruction would have filled differently.
//
// ⚠ **IT DECLINES MORE THAN IT STRICTLY MUST, DELIBERATELY.** `min`/`max` and `abs`/`fneg` hand back an
// OPERAND's pattern rather than computing one, so a NaN through those is the same on every target and
// could be folded safely. Telling them apart would need a generate-versus-select roster over two opcode
// enums — a fifth place the float band is written down — to buy a case NO PROGRAM CAN REACH: nothing in
// the language spells a NaN CONSTANT (there is no NaN literal, `__Builtins.bitsToFloat` is reserved, and
// the corpus's `inf - inf` idiom declines right here). One rule, no roster.
//
// The two REINTERPRETATIONS and `siToFp` do not come through here and must not: a bitcast computes
// nothing, so there is no machine choice to fear, and `siToFp` cannot produce a NaN from an integer.
//
// ⛔ **NO SPEC CASE CAN MEASURE THIS GUARD, AND THAT IS A PROPERTY OF THE THING RATHER THAN A GAP IN
// THE SUITE.** MEASURED 2026-08-30 with the decline removed: the whole suite stays green on x64 AND on
// wasm. The divergence is between two MACHINES' choices of payload, so staging it needs the compiler
// and the running program on DIFFERENT machines; and the one assertion a program can make about a NaN
// — `y != y` — is true of every payload. What sees it is `a-computed-nan-keeps-its-instruction`'s
// committed fragment, where the decline is the surviving `subsd`. Do not read the green suite as
// evidence that removing this is safe; read the fragment.
function foldedFloatConst(op StdOp, resultId ValueId, bits ParsedInt) returns StdOp
	if floatBitsAreNaN(bits) 'payloadIsTheMachinesToChoose'
		return op
	end 'payloadIsTheMachinesToChoose'

	return StdOp.const(resultId, value: bits, valueType: FoldedFloatType)
end 'foldedFloatConst'

// `resultId = lhs <opcode> rhs` over two constants — the DOMAIN SPLIT, and then whichever evaluator
// speaks for it. `operandType` is the type both operands are read at, so one test picks the tier.
function evaluateBinOp(op StdOp, resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, opcode StdBinOpcode, operandType StdType, constMap ConstValueMap) returns StdOp
	if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) 'float'
		return evaluateFloatBinOp(op, resultId: resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType, constMap: constMap)
	end 'float'

	if not operandsAreFoldableIntegers(constMap, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, operandType: operandType) 'notFoldable'
		return op
	end 'notFoldable'

	return match evaluateIntBinOp(opcode, lhs: constMap.constantOf(lhs), rhs: constMap.constantOf(rhs)) 'e'
		folded(value) gives StdOp.const(resultId, value: value, valueType: FoldedArithType)
		notFoldable gives op
	end 'e'
end 'evaluateBinOp'

// `resultId = lhs <opcode> rhs` over two float constants. Six opcodes reach it — `add`/`sub`/`mul`
// carried by the arithmetic band, and the three `stdBinOpcodeIsFloatOnly` members `div`/`min`/`max` —
// and `foldFloatBinOp` answers for all six, which is why there is no roster here.
//
// ⚠ **THE OPERANDS ARE PASSED IN SOURCE ORDER AND MUST STAY THAT WAY.** `div`, `min` and `max` are all
// `binOpcodeIsCommutative: false`, and for `min`/`max` the reason is the definition itself: a strict
// comparison is false on unordered input, so a swap changes the answer on a NaN.
//
// ⚠ **AN INTEGRAL OPCODE WITH A FLOAT `operandType` PANICS HERE RATHER THAN DECLINING, AND THAT IS A
// CHANGE OF DISPOSITION WORTH NAMING.** It used to be refused by the blanket float guard and left alone;
// now it reaches `foldFloatBinOp`, which panics because `mod`, the bit ops and the shifts have no float
// reading. That op is ILLEGAL IR and the panic is right — but note where the invariant actually lives:
// `requireBinOpOperands` gives E3005 for a float `mod`/bit-op/shift in the FRONT END, and
// `appendStdBinOp` guards only the other direction (a float-only opcode over an integer type). Nothing
// in the op constructor refuses this one, so the front end is the whole of the guarantee.
//
// EC18's `mulHighSigned` cannot arrive here either, and for a different reason: it is INTEGER-ONLY —
// `stdBinOpcodeIsFloatOnly` answers `false` for it, which is a claim about float-onlyness and not about
// integer-onlyness, so the fact worth citing is its own header ("there is no float 128-bit product").
// The guard that declines it is `evaluateIntBinOp`'s, on the path it can reach.
function evaluateFloatBinOp(op StdOp, resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, opcode StdBinOpcode, operandType StdType, constMap ConstValueMap) returns StdOp
	if not operandsAreFoldableFloats(constMap, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, operandType: operandType) 'notFoldable'
		return op
	end 'notFoldable'

	return match foldFloatBinOp(maxonBinOpOfStdOpcode(opcode), lhs: constMap.constantOf(lhs), rhs: constMap.constantOf(rhs)) 'e'
		folded(value) gives foldedFloatConst(op, resultId: resultId, bits: value)
		// The `±0.0` divisor — `foldFloatBinOp` rides the host's own divide-by-zero trap to get here.
		notFoldable gives op
	end 'e'
end 'evaluateFloatBinOp'

// `resultId = lhs <pred> rhs` over two constants, answering the 0/1 payload a `bool` value carries.
//
// TOTAL where `evaluateBinOp` is not: past the admission rule no comparison can decline
// (`foldIntCompare` has no `FoldedInt` to return), because there is no division to trap and no shift
// count to reject.
function evaluateCmp(op StdOp, resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, constMap ConstValueMap) returns StdOp
	// The float arm is asked BEFORE the unsigned decline below: a float is never unsigned
	// (`stdTypeIsUnsigned` is false for f64), so the order is not load-bearing today, but the float path
	// must not be reachable THROUGH a test about integer signedness.
	if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) 'float'
		return evaluateFloatCmp(op, resultId: resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, constMap: constMap)
	end 'float'

	if not operandsAreFoldableIntegers(constMap, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, operandType: operandType) 'notFoldable'
		return op
	end 'notFoldable'

	// ⛔⛔ **AN UNSIGNED ORDERING COMPARISON IS NOT THIS FOLDER'S TO ANSWER, AND THE PREMISE THAT SAID
	// OTHERWISE WAS FALSIFIED BY A CHANGE IN ANOTHER FILE.** This file's header used to argue that every
	// integer comparison in the language is SIGNED at 64 bits on all three targets, so the parser's
	// `foldIntCompare` — which is signed — was the whole answer. X11 made signedness a real distinction
	// carried on the op's `operandType` (`stdTypeIsUnsigned`, `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerCompare`), and one
	// bit pattern now has two honest readings: `u64.max` rides as the wrapped `-1`, so folding
	// `3 > u64.max` signed answers TRUE where the program's answer is FALSE.
	// MEASURED: `comparison-operators/compared-against-the-unsigned-extreme` returned 1 where it pins 42.
	// ⇒ Decline, rather than reach for a second comparison evaluator. That is the same rule this pass
	// already applies to `div`/`mod` and to floats: where one folder cannot speak for the operation, the
	// operation keeps its instruction.
	if stdTypeIsUnsigned(operandType) 'unsignedReading'
		return op
	end 'unsignedReading'

	let verdict = foldIntCompare(maxonCmpOpOfStdPred(pred), lhs: constMap.constantOf(lhs), rhs: constMap.constantOf(rhs))
	return StdOp.const(resultId, value: verdict, valueType: FoldedComparisonType)
end 'evaluateCmp'

// `resultId = lhs <pred> rhs` over two float constants — and it is THE ONE FLOAT FOLD WITH NO DECLINE AT
// ALL past the admission rule. `foldFloatCompare` is total and IEEE-correct (unordered is false for
// every predicate but `ne`, and `-0.0 == +0.0`), and its answer is the same i1 on every machine — so
// neither the NaN rule nor a width question touches the RESULT, whatever the operands were.
//
// ⭐ A folded float comparison is also what makes `foldConstantBranches` reach a float `if`: the
// condition becomes a `const` this pass already knows how to read, and the arm not taken is dropped.
function evaluateFloatCmp(op StdOp, resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, constMap ConstValueMap) returns StdOp
	if not operandsAreFoldableFloats(constMap, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, operandType: operandType) 'notFoldable'
		return op
	end 'notFoldable'

	let verdict = foldFloatCompare(maxonCmpOpOfStdPred(pred), lhs: constMap.constantOf(lhs), rhs: constMap.constantOf(rhs))
	return StdOp.const(resultId, value: verdict, valueType: FoldedComparisonType)
end 'evaluateFloatCmp'

// `resultId = <opcode> operand` over one constant. The evaluators are the Maxon tier's own, reached
// through the unary bridge exactly as `evaluateIntBinOp` reaches `foldIntBinOp`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SPLIT IS ON THE OPCODE, NOT ON `operandType`, AND THAT IS `StdUnaryOpcode`'s OWN RULE.**
// `stdUnaryOperandIsFloat` is the one place the question "what register file does this instruction's
// SOURCE come out of" is answered, and on the two conversions and the two bitcasts the operand's domain
// and the RESULT's differ — a `siToFp` reads an integer and answers a float, a `fpToSi` the reverse.
// Asking the opcode routes each to the folder that speaks for it; the operand TYPE is then asked by the
// admission rule on that side, which is also the check that the two agree (`LowerMaxonToStd`'s
// `assertUnaryOperandDomain` asserts the same agreement when the op is built, so a disagreement here is
// declined rather than diagnosed a second time).
//
// ⭐ **WHY THE INTEGER ARM EXISTS AT ALL:** `-K` over a top-level or inlined constant is a `neg` of a
// `const`, where the same value spelled `0 - K` is a `sub` of two — and the binary shape folded to one
// `mov` while the unary kept its instruction. The `0 - x` → `-x` sweep of 2026-08-30 made every such
// site the unary shape, and `top-level-let/unary-minus-in-constant`'s golden showed the cost in one line.
function evaluateUnaryOp(op StdOp, resultId ValueId, operand ValueId, opcode StdUnaryOpcode, operandType StdType, constMap ConstValueMap) returns StdOp
	if stdUnaryOperandIsFloat(opcode) 'floatSource'
		return evaluateFloatUnaryOp(op, resultId: resultId, operand: operand, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType, constMap: constMap)
	end 'floatSource'

	if not operandIsFoldableInteger(constMap, operand: operand, operandType: operandType) 'notFoldable'
		return op
	end 'notFoldable'

	return evaluateIntSourceUnaryOp(op, resultId: resultId, opcode: opcode, bits: constMap.constantOf(operand))
end 'evaluateUnaryOp'

// `<opcode> const` where the OPERAND is an integer — `neg`/`bitNot`/`logicalNot`, the sign-extension
// family, and the two ops that leave the integer domain.
//
// ⭐ **THREE OPCODES ARE ANSWERED HERE BECAUSE ONLY THIS TIER HOLDS WHAT SETTLES THEM**, and they are
// three different "only":
//
//   • `siToFp` and `bitcastI64ToF64` — `foldIntUnaryOp` declines both, and it is RIGHT to, for the reason
//     its own arms give: a `ParsedInt` column that cannot say which of its entries is a bit pattern may
//     not assert of one that it holds an encoding. A Std op carries `valueType`, so this pass CAN. The
//     first is a real conversion, computed once in `TypeRules.foldSiToFp` (the parser's own promotion
//     calls the same function, so `1 + 2.0` in a `let` and `1 + 2.0` past the inliner cannot disagree);
//     the second is a REINTERPRETATION whose fold is the identity on the payload — the same `ParsedInt`,
//     re-minted at f64. Neither goes through `foldedFloatConst`, deliberately: an integer has no NaN to
//     carry into a conversion, and a bitcast asked for exactly those bits.
//   • ⭐⭐ **`logicalNot` — because THE OPCODE IS WHICH `not` THE SOURCE MEANT, and only this tier has
//     it.** `TypeRules` has TWO `not` folders and its own rule for choosing between them
//     (`notOperatorFor`): `foldIntUnaryOp` is the INTEGER one and answers `notFoldable` for
//     `logicalNot`, because on an integer that operator is a parser bug; `foldLogicalNot` is the BOOL
//     one and is total. At the Maxon tier the choice needs a type. Here it does not — `StdUnaryOpcode`
//     already SPLIT the two operators into two opcodes at lowering, so the question the parser answers
//     with `notOperatorFor` is answered by which arm this `match` takes.
//     ⇒ Routing `logicalNot` to the integer folder was a MISSED FOLD — and this pass is what makes it
//     reachable, because nothing else in the compiler folds a COMPARISON, so `not (2 > 1)` had a
//     constant operand only once `evaluateCmp` had made one. MEASURED 2026-08-31: that program now
//     compiles to NO INSTRUCTIONS AT ALL (the `not` folds, then `foldConstantBranches` takes the arm and
//     `dropUnreachableBlocks` removes the other); before, it kept an `xor`, a `cmp` and a `jcc` on a
//     condition known at compile time. Found in review.
//     ⚠ It is NOT what leaves the `xor`/`cmp` pairs in `a-comparison-on-float-constants-folds`' own
//     fragment: those `not`s read a leaf call's result, which arrives as a BLOCK ARG of `__il_cont`, and
//     this pass is deliberately not a constant propagator. Two different boundaries, one symptom.
function evaluateIntSourceUnaryOp(op StdOp, resultId ValueId, opcode StdUnaryOpcode, bits ParsedInt) returns StdOp
	if opcode == StdUnaryOpcode.siToFp 'promotion'
		return StdOp.const(resultId, value: foldSiToFp(bits), valueType: FoldedFloatType)
	end 'promotion'

	if opcode == StdUnaryOpcode.bitcastI64ToF64 'reinterpretation'
		return StdOp.const(resultId, value: bits, valueType: FoldedFloatType)
	end 'reinterpretation'

	// The result is a BOOL, so it is minted at `FoldedComparisonType` and not at `FoldedArithType` —
	// which is also what the wasm tier declares for this opcode unconditionally (`unaryResultValType`
	// answers i32 for `logicalNot` whatever its `operandType` is, because `emitLogicalNotExpr` emits an
	// `i32.xor`). Reading the operand's own type here instead would be the trap that table warns about.
	if opcode == StdUnaryOpcode.logicalNot 'boolNot'
		return StdOp.const(resultId, value: foldLogicalNot(bits), valueType: FoldedComparisonType)
	end 'boolNot'

	return match foldIntUnaryOp(maxonUnaryOpOfStdOpcode(opcode), operand: bits) 'e'
		folded(value) gives StdOp.const(resultId, value: value, valueType: FoldedArithType)
		notFoldable gives op
	end 'e'
end 'evaluateIntSourceUnaryOp'

// `<opcode> const` where the OPERAND is a float — the four math intrinsics, the two sign operations, the
// truncation and the outbound bitcast. Three answers with three minted types, which is the whole reason
// the arms are separate rather than one call:
//
//   • `fpToSi` answers an INTEGER, and only inside the `i64` range (`TypeRules.foldFpToSi` holds the
//     range and the reason the three targets disagree outside it).
//   • `bitcastF64ToI64` answers the SAME BITS in the integer domain — the identity on the payload, and
//     `float.hash`'s whole implementation.
//   • `floor`/`ceil`/`round`/`sqrt`/`abs`/`fneg` answer a FLOAT, through `foldedFloatConst` so the NaN
//     rule is asked once.
function evaluateFloatUnaryOp(op StdOp, resultId ValueId, operand ValueId, opcode StdUnaryOpcode, operandType StdType, constMap ConstValueMap) returns StdOp
	if not operandIsFoldableFloat(constMap, operand: operand, operandType: operandType) 'notFoldable'
		return op
	end 'notFoldable'

	let bits = constMap.constantOf(operand)

	if opcode == StdUnaryOpcode.fpToSi 'truncation'
		return match foldFpToSi(bits) 'e'
			folded(value) gives StdOp.const(resultId, value: value, valueType: FoldedArithType)
			notFoldable gives op
		end 'e'
	end 'truncation'

	if opcode == StdUnaryOpcode.bitcastF64ToI64 'reinterpretation'
		return StdOp.const(resultId, value: bits, valueType: FoldedArithType)
	end 'reinterpretation'

	return match foldFloatUnaryOp(maxonUnaryOpOfStdOpcode(opcode), operand: bits) 'e'
		folded(value) gives foldedFloatConst(op, resultId: resultId, bits: value)
		notFoldable gives op
	end 'e'
end 'evaluateFloatUnaryOp'

// ⭐⭐ **THE FOLDED CONSTANT'S TYPE IS THE MACHINE WORD, NOT THE OP'S `operandType`, AND ON wasm THE
// DIFFERENCE IS A MISCOMPILE.** A `binOp`'s RESULT is declared at `wasmMachineValTypeOf(operandType)` —
// `i64` for every integer type — while a `const`'s is declared at `wasmValTypeOf(valueType)`, which is
// `i32` for `i8`/`i16`/`i32`/`u8`/`u16`/`u32`. Carrying the op's own narrow `operandType` onto the
// replacement `const` would therefore CHANGE THE VALUE'S LOCAL CLASS from i64 to i32 in the middle of a
// function, and every later read of it would pop the wrong type. `i64` is not a widening here — it is
// what the operation already computed at, on every target.
let FoldedArithType = StdType.i64

// A comparison's result is the i1 the `cmp` it replaces declared, which is what keeps its wasm local an
// i32 (`recordDefValtype` types every `cmp` result i32, and `wasmValTypeOf(i1)` is i32).
let FoldedComparisonType = StdType.i1

// ⭐⭐ **THE FLOAT MEMBER OF THE SAME RULE — "the RESULT's domain at its machine word" — AND THE ONE
// PLACE THE `f64`-ONLY-NESS OF THIS PASS'S FLOAT PATH IS WRITTEN DOWN.** Read it in both directions:
//
//   • **As a MINTED type**, it is what a float-valued fold carries. It needs no widening argument, for
//     the reason `FoldedArithType` needed one: a float is the single `StdType` where
//     `wasmMachineValTypeOf` and `wasmValTypeOf` AGREE (`wasmMachineValTypeOf` answers
//     `wasmValTypeOf(t)` for a float and `i64` for everything else), so the class a `binOp` declared and
//     the class a `const` declares are the same f64 local and no value changes class mid-function. On
//     x64 and arm64 it is also the flag that routes `lowerConst` into `lowerFloatConst` and the `.rdata`
//     pool, which is where a float constant has to go — SSE and NEON have no float immediate.
//   • **As an ADMISSION test** (`operandIsFoldableFloat`), it is the width `TypeRules`' float evaluators
//     compute at. They run on the host's native f64, so an `f32` — spellable in `StdType`, minted
//     nowhere — must not reach them. One constant answers both, so the folder cannot come to compute at
//     a width the mint does not declare.
//
// ⛔ **NEVER MINT A FLOAT ANSWER AT `FoldedArithType`, AND THE COST IS NOT A WRONG NUMBER.** The value
// lands in the wrong register file: `x64 emitter: rax is in the gpr register file where the xmm file is
// required` (MEASURED 2026-08-28), and on wasm an i64 local that every `f64.*` consumer fails validation
// against. That is the sabotage `specs-shv2/fold-constants.md`'s float control pins.
let FoldedFloatType = StdType.f64

// Evaluate `lhs <opcode> rhs` at the Std tier by asking THE PARSER'S FOLDER — see this file's header for
// why there is no second evaluator.
//
// ⚠ **THE SHIFTS DO NOT GO THROUGH `foldIntBinOp`, AND THAT IS THAT FUNCTION'S OWN RULE RATHER THAN AN
// EXCEPTION TO IT.** It answers `notFoldable` for every shift deliberately: a shift's rule is
// `evalShift`'s and needs its COUNT checked first. So the count is checked here — against
// `shiftCountIsUnguarded`, the same window `constFitsBinOpImmediate` folds an immediate shift count
// into, and the window on which the imm8 form, the `cl` form and `evalShift` provably agree because the
// hardware's mask is a no-op on it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A COUNT OUTSIDE THE WINDOW IS LEFT ALONE, AND A NEGATIVE ONE IS WHY — SABOTAGE-MEASURED, NOT
// ASSUMED.** The parser refuses a NEGATIVE literal count (E2054) and rewrites an out-of-range one, so
// before EC5 no such constant could exist at all. `inlineLeaves` makes one: it substitutes a caller's
// literal into a callee whose count was a PARAMETER, and for a parameter the parser emitted
// `emitGuardedShift`'s saturation cascade rather than a diagnostic. **MEASURED 2026-08-28** with this
// gate removed and `shiftLeft(1, n: -1)` compiled through such a leaf:
//
//     panic at TypeRules.maxon:1964: evalShift: negative count -1 — a negative count is E2054 and must
//     never reach the folder
//
// i.e. the compiler DIES. (A negative literal does reach this pass as a single `const`, not as a `neg`
// of one.) `specs-shv2/fold-constants.md`'s shift case carries both signs for exactly that reason.
//
// ⚠ **AN OUT-OF-RANGE POSITIVE COUNT IS A DIFFERENT AND MILDER STORY, AND SAYING SO IS THE HONEST
// VERSION.** For counts of 64 and above `evalShift` SATURATES to the same value the emitted cascade
// computes, so folding one would be correct — the same sabotage leaves every positive case GREEN.
// The window is still the right gate: it is the one `constFitsBinOpImmediate` already folds a shift
// immediate against, and asking it once covers both signs instead of adding a second negative-only
// test that would have to be kept in step. Declining costs one unfolded shift inside a cascade that is
// already six ops.
function evaluateIntBinOp(opcode StdBinOpcode, lhs ParsedInt, rhs ParsedInt) returns FoldedInt
	// ⚠ **EC18's HIGH MULTIPLY IS DECLINED BEFORE THE BRIDGE, NOT INSIDE IT.** Two independent facts
	// say no and it needs only the first: its answer is bits 64..127 of a 128-bit product, which a
	// 64-bit `ParsedInt` cannot hold, so this pass has no arithmetic for it; and it has no `MaxonBinOp`
	// twin at all, so `maxonBinOpOfStdOpcode` PANICS rather than answering. The guard is therefore ahead
	// of the bridge. It is unreachable today — `StrengthReduceDivision` is scheduled AFTER this pass, so
	// no `mulHighSigned` exists in the module when it runs — and it is written anyway, because "the
	// schedule keeps it away" is exactly the kind of premise a later reordering falsifies silently.
	if opcode == StdBinOpcode.mulHighSigned 'noHostArithmeticAndNoSourceOperator'
		return FoldedInt.notFoldable
	end 'noHostArithmeticAndNoSourceOperator'

	let op = maxonBinOpOfStdOpcode(opcode)

	if shiftOperator(op) 'shift'
		if not shiftCountIsUnguarded(rhs) 'outsideTheWindow'
			return FoldedInt.notFoldable
		end 'outsideTheWindow'

		return FoldedInt.folded(evalShift(lhs, count: rhs, op: op))
	end 'shift'

	return foldIntBinOp(op, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs)
end 'evaluateIntBinOp'

// Rewrite every `condBranch` whose condition is a known constant into the unconditional `branch` its
// condition already decided.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE ARM THAT CAN NO LONGER BE REACHED IS LEFT WHERE IT IS, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN.** Removing a
// block is `EC11`'s `dropUnreachableBlocks` (Target tier, after `applyAllocation`), which does it
// transitively and at the point where a block's emptiness is finally a fact rather than a prediction.
// v1's `Canonicalize` makes exactly the same split — "dead block removal is left to the DCE pass" — and
// an unreachable block is already an ordinary state of this IR (the parser mints `Terminator.dead`
// blocks for code after a fully-terminated `if`/`else`). So a constant `if` costs one branch here and
// nothing at all by the time the code is encoded.
function foldConstantBranches(module StdModule, func IrFunction, constMap ConstValueMap) returns bool
	var foldedAny = false

	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		var block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("foldConstants: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

		if not block.hasTerminatorOp() 'noTerminator'
			continue
		end 'noTerminator'

		let termIdx = block.terminatorIndex()
		let term = terminatorOpAt(module, termIdx: termIdx)
		if foldConstantBranchIn(module, block: block, termIdx: termIdx, term: term, constMap: constMap) 'folded'
			foldedAny = true
		end 'folded'
	end 'eachBlock'

	return foldedAny
end 'foldConstantBranches'

// ⚠ **READ THROUGH A FUNCTION, NOT INLINE, AND THAT IS E3070 RATHER THAN TIDINESS.** A
// `module.ops.get(...)` written inline borrows `module` for as long as the value lives, and the fold in
// the very next breath MUTATES it (`setTerminator`) — which the SELF-COMPILE refuses and the bootstrap
// does not. Reading through a function ends the borrow at the return, the same discipline
// `LeafInliner.opAtIndex` and `PrimitiveInliner.opAt` state for themselves.
function terminatorOpAt(module StdModule, termIdx OpIndex) returns StdOp
	return try module.ops.get(termIdx) otherwise panic("foldConstants: terminator index {termIdx} not in module.ops")
end 'terminatorOpAt'

// Fold ONE block's terminator, when it is a `condBranch` on a value this pass knows.
function foldConstantBranchIn(module StdModule, block IrBlock, termIdx OpIndex, term StdOp, constMap ConstValueMap) returns bool
	return match term 'term'
		// Everything BEFORE the conditional branch in the union — the arith band. None is a terminator at
		// all, so none can reach here; the arm exists because the match must be exhaustive.
		const to mod gives false
		condBranch(condId, thenBlockId, elseBlockId) gives foldCondBranch(module, block: block, termIdx: termIdx, condId: condId, thenBlockId: thenBlockId, elseBlockId: elseBlockId, constMap: constMap)
		// Every other terminator — `branch`, `ret`, `retVoid`, `errorReturn`, `switch`, `osExit` — carries
		// no condition to know. ⚠ `switch` is the one with a foldable SUBJECT (a constant index selects a
		// fixed arm); it is a jump-table fold, not a branch fold, and is left for the rung that takes it.
		branch to osEnterBackgroundPriority gives false
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable gives false
		osEnvBlock to osEnvBlockFree gives false
	end 'term'
end 'foldConstantBranchIn'

// ⚠ **A `condBranch` WHOSE TWO TARGETS ARE THE SAME BLOCK IS REFUSED.** Both edges name that block, and
// after the rewrite exactly one of them must survive — but which one is a question about the ARGUMENTS
// each arm passes to the successor's phis, and an edge carries no arm label to tell them apart. v1's
// `Canonicalize` folds this shape on the reading that a dead predicate makes the arms equivalent; that is
// true of the DCE-collapsed condBr it was written for and is NOT true in general, so shv2 declines rather
// than pick an arm by position. (Nothing in the corpus produces it: the parser mints a distinct block per
// arm.)
function foldCondBranch(module StdModule, block IrBlock, termIdx OpIndex, condId ValueId, thenBlockId BlockId, elseBlockId BlockId, constMap ConstValueMap) returns bool
	if thenBlockId == elseBlockId 'oneTargetTwoEdges'
		return false
	end 'oneTargetTwoEdges'

	if not constMap.contains(condId) 'conditionNotKnown'
		return false
	end 'conditionNotKnown'

	// The condition is an i1 the `cmp` fold above wrote as 0 or 1, or a literal `true`/`false` the parser
	// materialized. Anything non-zero takes the then-arm, which is what `condBranch` means.
	let conditionHolds = constMap.constantOf(condId) != ConditionIsFalse
	let taken = thenBlockId if conditionHolds else elseBlockId
	let dropped = elseBlockId if conditionHolds else thenBlockId

	try module.ops.set(termIdx, value: StdOp.branch(taken)) otherwise panic("foldConstants: ops.set OOB at terminator index {termIdx}")
	block.branchEdges = edgesWithoutTarget(block.branchEdges, droppedTarget: dropped)

	logTrace(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "foldConstants: condBranch on a constant -> branch block_{taken} (block_{dropped} loses this predecessor)")

	return true
end 'foldCondBranch'

// The i1 payload a FALSE condition carries. Named because it is a fact about the bool encoding
// (`TypeRules.boolPayload`) rather than a number that happens to appear here.
let ConditionIsFalse = 0 as ParsedInt

// `edges` without the ONE edge to `droppedTarget` — the arm the fold made unreachable from this block.
//
// ⚠ A FRESH ARRAY, NEVER AN IN-PLACE REMOVAL. `IrBlock`'s header states the rule and the reason: the phi
// arrays are SHARED all the way back into the parse memo, so mutating one corrupts every later compile of
// the project, and nothing downstream re-reads the Maxon or Std phi model to catch it.
//
// It removes at most ONE edge even though `branchEdges` is not keyed — a block records an edge per
// successor that CARRIES PHIS, so the not-taken target has either one edge here or none (the same-target
// case, where it could have two, is refused by `foldCondBranch`).
function edgesWithoutTarget(edges BranchEdgeArray, droppedTarget BlockId) returns BranchEdgeArray
	var kept = BranchEdgeArray.create()
	var alreadyDropped = false

	for edge in edges 'eachEdge'
		if not alreadyDropped and edge.targetBlockId == droppedTarget 'theNotTakenEdge'
			alreadyDropped = true
			continue
		end 'theNotTakenEdge'

		kept.push(edge)
	end 'eachEdge'

	return kept
end 'edgesWithoutTarget'

// ⭐⭐ **DROP EVERY BLOCK THIS FUNCTION CAN NO LONGER REACH — AND THIS PASS MUST DO IT ITSELF, WHICH IS
// NOT WHAT THE ROW'S PLAN SAID.** The plan was to leave dead-block removal to `EC11`'s Target-tier
// `dropUnreachableBlocks`, exactly as v1's `Canonicalize` leaves it to v1's DCE. **MEASURED 2026-08-28:
// that breaks the register allocator**, on `match x` over a constant subject
// (`specs-shv2/match-statements.md` `match-statements.function-call`):
//
//     panic at RegisterAllocator.maxon:1504: seedInUse: value 18 is live-in to block 11 but was never
//     colored — a use dominates its def (structured-layout invariant broken)
//
// ⇒ **THE HAZARD IS NOT THE UNREACHABLE BLOCK. IT IS AN UNREACHABLE BLOCK THAT FEEDS A PHI.** Before
// this pass, the only unreachable Std blocks were the parser's `dead` continuations, which have no
// predecessor AND no branch edge — so nothing downstream had ever met one that passed a value to a live
// block's `blockArgs`. Folding a `condBranch` makes one: the arm not taken keeps its edge into the merge
// block, and the value it passes is defined in code no path reaches. `reorderFuncBlocksRpo` then APPENDS
// every unreached block after the RPO ("they still hold ops that get emitted"), so that definition now
// sits AFTER the merge block that uses it in layout order — which is precisely the structured-layout
// invariant the allocator asserts. The allocator is right and the IR was wrong.
//
// ⇒ **A PASS THAT ORPHANS A BLOCK OWNS THE ORPHAN.** Removing it here removes its `branchEdges` with it,
// which is the only thing that repairs the merge block's phi — nothing downstream can, because by the
// time the allocator sees the function that edge looks exactly like a real one.
//
// It still composes with `EC11` rather than duplicating it: that pass drops what the TARGET tier
// orphans — its own jump threading, and any block only unreachable once physical branches are elided —
// which is a different set, discovered at a different time. This one is the Std tier's, and it runs only
// on a function where a branch actually folded.
//
// Survivors keep their relative order, so nothing that depends on layout moves.
//
// ⚠ **A BLOCK WITH NO TERMINATOR OP FALLS THROUGH PHYSICALLY, AND THE OPS-DERIVED CFG CANNOT SEE THAT
// EDGE** — `EC11`'s finding, restated here because this walk has the same hole and takes the same cure:
// the physical edge is added explicitly, so dropping the block a live one falls into cannot silently
// re-point it at whatever survived further down.
function dropUnreachableBlocks(module StdModule, func IrFunction)
	let blockCount = func.blockRefs.count()
	if blockCount == 0 'noBlocks'
		return
	end 'noBlocks'

	let byId = module.blockIndexById(func)
	var reachable = BoolArray.create()
	reachable.growFilled(blockCount, value: false)
	var stack = BlockRefIndexArray.create()
	var successors = BlockIdArray.create()

	// The entry is position 0 and is reachable by definition — it is also the block that must stay
	// first, which the ascending rebuild below preserves.
	try reachable.set(0, value: true) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: reachable.set OOB at the entry block")
	stack.push(0)

	while stack.count() > 0 'walk'
		let position = try stack.pop() otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: stack.pop on a non-empty stack failed")
		let blockRef = try func.blockRefs.get(position) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: blockRefs.get OOB at {position}")
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

		successors.clear()
		collectStdBlockSuccessorIds(module, block: block, out: successors)

		for successorId in successors 'eachSucc'
			markReachable(reachable, stack: stack, position: try byId.get(successorId) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: block {successorId} is a branch target of this function but is not in its blockRefs"))
		end 'eachSucc'

		if not block.hasTerminatorOp() and position + 1 < blockCount 'fallsThroughPhysically'
			markReachable(reachable, stack: stack, position: position + 1)
		end 'fallsThroughPhysically'
	end 'walk'

	var kept = BlockRefArray.create()
	for position in 0 upto blockCount 'eachBlock'
		let survives = try reachable.get(position) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: reachable.get OOB at {position}")
		if survives 'keep'
			kept.push(try func.blockRefs.get(position) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: blockRefs.get OOB at {position}"))
		end 'keep'
	end 'eachBlock'

	func.blockRefs = kept
end 'dropUnreachableBlocks'

// The worklist of `func.blockRefs` POSITIONS the reachability walk still owes a visit.
typealias BlockRefIndexArray = Array with BlockRefIndex

// Mark a block position reachable and queue it, once. Both columns are the caller's.
function markReachable(reachable BoolArray, stack BlockRefIndexArray, position BlockRefIndex)
	let seenBefore = try reachable.get(position) otherwise panic("markReachable: reachable.get OOB at {position}")
	if seenBefore 'alreadySeen'
		return
	end 'alreadySeen'

	try reachable.set(position, value: true) otherwise panic("markReachable: reachable.set OOB at {position}")
	stack.push(position)
end 'markReachable'
